  {
    "episode_id": 90465,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90465.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 193,
    "turn_text": "Well, I wouldn't say yet, but he certainly has sent warning signs where he's essentially, at one time or another, reduce the ability to have access to scientific information. The taxpayers paid for, by the way, seems to me if taxpayers pay for it, we should have access to climate science available to us. He has clearly rolled back the Obama administration rules, the clean energy rule, to reduce carbon from coal. He has, I'm sure, part of the cabal that refused to join the whole rest of the nation, of the nations of the world in Paris, to commit to action. He certainly has not helped in any way, so I can't think of a positive thing to say that the EPA has done to help in this regard. And pulling out of this Paris agreement, you couldn't think of of anything more sort of backward leaning. And I can't think of sort of a more un-American thing. We've always led the world. We led the world against fascism and communism. The whole world has looked to us for leadership as the last bastion of liberty. We've led the world in scientific achievement. We're one of the most economically rich countries in the world. And yet to be at the very back, the caboose, not even on the caboose of the international train, is an international embarrassment and an environmental disaster in the making if we don't reverse this course. So I believe all of us has to be dedicated to this as I've indicated my state is and we've had some great successes and I intend to continue that and every time a state has success other states become a little more",
