  {
    "episode_id": 90465,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90465.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 199,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the injustice, the innate inhumanity, the lack of empathy shown by the president in this case speaks for itself. It's difficult to think of how duplicative the president has been where essentially we offered these dreamers an opportunity that if you step forward and share your name and your address and say you're going to go to school and keep your nose clean and we'll make sure you continue building the American dream here and then to pull the rug out from under them after Uncle Sam has told him they're going to do that the injustice of that is palpable and And the human, I've had this experience because we worked for years to pass our version of the Dream Act in Washington State. And this was a bill that would give these dreamers financial aid like people who were born in Washington State. And we worked for years on this, and the Republicans were not helpful for some period of time. But then we had several hundred of these dreamers come down in Olympia and really talked to legislators and I learned some of those meetings. When these young people look at you in the eye and you see the spark of ambition and their future, it's something something shines in your heart. If you have one, say, I got to help this person. And I saw that. So we passed on a bipartisan basis, our DREAM Act, so they can make sure that they get financial aid. That is now threatened because the president wants to take that right away from them",
